Committed as eebba50. Summary:

app/services/certificate_service.py:
- maybe_issue_certificate(conn, student_id, level_id) — checks whether the student has now passed every published test in every published material of the level; if so and no certificate exists yet, marks student_levels COMPLETED,
  generates a unique certificate number (ILC-{level}-{student}-{random}), and inserts the certificatevel isn't complete or was already certified (idempotent — retaking a passed test doesn't re-issue).
- send_certificate_email_and_log(conn, certificate) — sends the congratulations email via the existing SMTP service and logs the outcome (SENT/FAILED) to the notifications table, run after the issuing transaction commits (network
  calls don't belong inside a DB transaction).

Hooked into submit_attempt (app/api/testing.py): after a passing submission, checks for level complete + sends the notification, following the same "commit DB writes first, then do the network call"pattern used elsewhere in this codebase.

New endpoints (app/api/certificates.py): GET /certificates/me (student's own), GET /certificates/{id} (owner or staff), and a public GET /certificates/verify/{certificate_number} for third-party credential verification.

Tested end-to-end: passing only 1 of 2 required tests issues nothing; passing the second auto-issues the certificate, flips student_levels to COMPLETED, and the email genuinely sent via Gmail (confirmed via the notifications row:
status: SENT); retaking an already-passed test doesn't create a duplicate certificate; verification bd 404s on a bogus one; cross-student certificate access is blocked (403). All test data cleaned upafterward.

